  "from_addr": "Mike Allen <mike@axios.com>",
  "subject": "🛶 Axios Finish Line: Glacial life lessons",
  "date": "Thu, 10 Sep 2026 21:29:24 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 👵 Plus: Ask Nana | Thursday, September 10, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Sep 10, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n It's almost Friday! Today's guest host is Axios national energy correspondent Amy Harder, reflecting on what she learned during a recent trip to Alaska with her husband.\n\n Smart Brevity™ count: 528 words … 2 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Life lessons among Alaska&#x27;s glaciers \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Axios' Amy Harder kayaking on Spencer Glacier lake, Alaska, in August 2026. That glacier might look close, but it's nearly 2 miles away. Photo: Amy Harder/Axios SPENCER GLACIER, Alaska — Sure, seeing is believing, but sometimes seeing leaves you in disbelief. That happened to me while kayaking among icebergs here recently, as the landscape transformed before my eyes, Amy Harder writes. \n\n Why it matters: I began the kayaking excursion certain of what I was seeing. I ended it with my mind blown — by the sheer scale of nature and by how much perspectives can change. 🌊 State of play: The glacial lake here, not far from Anchorage, is accessible only by the Alaska Railroad train .\n\n The glacier and lake looked rather small as we approached it on foot with our kayaks in tow. I felt a tinge of disappointment. I figured the glacier was maybe a half-mile away, with dainty-looking icebergs in between us. Then our guide told us it was 2 miles away. No way, I said, as confident as ever. \"I'm fine being wrong,\" the guide joked with equal parts politeness and sarcasm.\n\n Once we got into the water and started paddling, my entire field of vision changed dramatically in an extreme manifestation of \"motion parallax.\" I soon realized how small I was, how big those icebergs were and how far away that glacier really was. 🏔️ How it works: \"Motion parallax\" is a common phenomenon we experience when moving. It's why trees whiz past us while driving but mountains in the distance barely seem to move. Glacial landscapes can put this phenomenon — and our struggles to perceive depth and scale — on steroids.\n\n From shore, nearly everything in my field of vision was enormous — mountains, ice, water — leaving me with few familiar visual cues to grasp just how big or far away anything really was. When we started paddling, movement — and our kayaks themselves — gave my brain new information.\n\n Despite paddling quite a ways, the glacier remained stubbornly distant, while those dainty-looking icebergs grew to the size of cars and small buildings. A seagull showing just how big this iceberg is on Spencer Glacier lake in Alaska, August 2026. Photo: Amy Harder/Axios 👀 The big picture: It was strangely empowering to be so wrong. I was confident in what I was seeing, and then my guide — and eventually my own eyes — showed me otherwise.\n\n That sense of wonder has lingered, making me curious about what else I might be so sure about — and wrong about. It was also a reminder that, from a distance, people and problems can look smaller, simpler and more obvious than they really are. Get closer, learn more and add context, and the picture can change dramatically. The bottom line: Sometimes changing your mind isn't a sign that you saw things wrong. It's a sign that you're finally seeing more.\n\n Share this story . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n Free Live Open House: See Smart Brevity in Action \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Communication breaks down when it's unclear. Join our free live Open House to see how Smart Brevity sharpens every update your team sends.\n\n Live demo of the Smart Brevity method Real before/after comms makeovers Q&A with an Axios HQ coach 👉 Save your spot .\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 📞 Call grandma and grandpa! \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n It's National Grandparents Day on Sunday, a chance to celebrate the unique grandparent-grandchild bond.\n\n For our young Finish Line readers: Here's a list, via Time Magazine , of 12 questions to ask your grandparent — or another source of wisdom in your life, like an aunt, uncle, godparent, teacher or coach. Happy weekend! \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
