954\"><img src=\"https://link.axios.com/img/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94as8x0r.7q5m/81e61136.gif\" alt=\"\" border=\"0\" /></a></td>\r\n<td style=\"padding-right:3.5%\" align=\"right\"> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47445291.360490/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tL25ld3NsZXR0ZXJzL2F4aW9zLWZpbmlzaC1saW5lLWUxNjMzNWJiLTlmNDktNDM0Ny05NGExLTU0YjI5MmY3ZjQ3NC5odG1sP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB0f01a023\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"b\" style=\"color:#222;font-size:13px;font-family:'gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;text-decoration:underline\">View in browser</a> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"30\" style=\"height:30px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>  <tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.3\"> </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td height=\"16\" style=\"height:16px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1\">\r\n<span class=\"s\" style=\"font-size:44px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#008dc8;letter-spacing:-1px;line-height:1\"> Axios Finish Line </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.5;padding-bottom:35px;padding-top:10px\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#222;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">By  Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei  <span style=\"margin:0 6px\">&middot;</span> Sep 10, 2026 </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;line-height:1.75;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;padding:0 3.5%\"><p><strong>It's almost Friday! </strong>Today's guest host is Axios national energy correspondent Amy Harder, reflecting on what she learned during a recent trip to Alaska with her husband.</p><ul><li><em>Smart Brevity™ count: 528 words … 2 mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern.</em></li></ul></td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Life lessons among Alaska&#x27;s glaciers </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/TNKs72YJXapHg7diifZG9Y2CXkU=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/10/1789072659822.png' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='632' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/TNKs72YJXapHg7diifZG9Y2CXkU=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/10/1789072659822.png\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">Axios' Amy Harder kayaking on Spencer Glacier lake, Alaska, in August 2026. That glacier might look close, but it's nearly 2 miles away. Photo: Amy Harder/Axios</div><p><strong>SPENCER GLACIER, Alaska </strong>— Sure, seeing is believing, but sometimes seeing leaves you in disbelief. That happened to me while kayaking among icebergs here recently, as the landscape transformed before my eyes, <em>Amy Harder writes.</em></p><ul><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> I began the kayaking excursion certain of what I was seeing. I ended it with my mind blown — by the sheer scale of nature and by how much perspectives can change.</li></ul><p><strong>🌊 State of play: </strong>The glacial lake here, not far from Anchorage, is accessible only by the <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47445291.\" target=\"_blank\">Alaska Railroad train</a>.</p><ul><li><strong>The glacier and lake</strong> looked rather small as we approached it on foot with our kayaks in tow. I felt a tinge of disappointment.</li><li><strong>I figured the glacier </strong>was maybe a half-mile away, with dainty-looking icebergs in between us. Then our guide told us it was 2 miles away. No way, I said, as confident as ever.</li></ul><p><strong>\"I'm fine being wrong,\"</strong> the guide joked with equal parts politeness and sarcasm.</p><ul><li><strong>Once we got into</strong> the water and started paddling, my entire field of vision changed dramatically in an extreme manifestation of \"motion parallax.\" I soon realized how small I was, how big those icebergs were and how far away that glacier really was.</li></ul><p><strong>🏔️ How it works:</strong> \"Motion parallax\" is a <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47445291.360490/aHR0cHM6Ly9qb3YuYXJ2b2pvdXJuYWxzLm9yZy9hcnRpY2xlLmFzcHg_YXJ0aWNsZWlkPTI1NDEzODEmdXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB962a7bca\" target=\"_blank\">common phenomenon</a> we experience when moving. It's why trees whiz past us while driving but mountains in the distance barely seem to move. Glacial landscapes can put this phenomenon — and our struggles to perceive depth and scale — on steroids.</p><ul><li><strong>From shore,</strong> nearly everything in my field of vision was enormous — mountains, ice, water — leaving me with few familiar visual cues to grasp just how big or far away anything really was.</li></ul><p><strong>When we started paddling,</strong> movement — and our kayaks themselves — gave my brain new information.</p><ul><li><strong>Despite paddling</strong> quite a ways, the glacier remained stubbornly distant, while those dainty-looking icebergs grew to the size of cars and small buildings.</li></ul><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/1TWewonmpaFYnI7Zg1B_QbKTDYQ=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/10/1789073190695.jpeg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='589' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/1TWewonmpaFYnI7Zg1B_QbKTDYQ=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/09/10/1789073190695.jpeg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">A seagull showing just how big this iceberg is on Spencer Glacier lake in Alaska, August 2026. Photo: Amy Harder/Axios</div><p><strong>👀 The big picture:</strong> It was strangely empowering to be so wrong. I was confident in what I was seeing, and then my guide — and eventually my own eyes — showed me otherwise.</p><ul><li><strong>That sense of wonder </strong>has lingered, making me curious about what else I might be so sure about — and wrong about.</li><li><strong>It was also a reminder </strong>that, from a distance, people and problems can look smaller, simpler and more obvious than they really are. Get closer, learn more and add context, and the picture can change dramatically.</li></ul><p><strong>The bottom line:</strong> Sometimes changing your mind isn't a sign that you saw things wrong. It's a sign that you're finally seeing more.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47445291.360490/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuYXhpb3MuY29tLzIwMjYvMDkvMTEvYWxhc2thLWdsYWNpZXJzLWxpZmUtbGVzc29ucz91dG1fc291cmNlPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXImdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB61dd83cb\" target=\"_blank\"><em>Share this story</em></a><em>.</em></li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://li